A||N||2||puter2k||88 gt stock wing mounting info||||1||
Z||000000||puter2k||03-14-2001||07:50 PM||||Is there anyone out there, that could give me the exact mounting hole locations, for the rear stock wing on an 88 GT ? I have an 88 GT that didn't come with a wing, and have been able to aquire an 88 OEM wing to put on it. I'm wanting to keep it ( factory stock ) for a car show coming soon.||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||03-14-2001||10:24 PM||||I think all the mounting locations are the same for any year with a wing, but someone might want to clarify this (i dont have a winged Fiero)<P>Check this out from the michigan fiero club website, I think it has all the info that you are looking for:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.michiganfieroclub.org/spoiler.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.michiganfieroclub.org/spoiler.htm</A> <P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phantom Rage||03-15-2001||07:04 AM||||I ran across the same problem with my 88 coupe, without much reply and no definite answers.  Check out this old post for pic.<BR>   <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007749.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007749.html</A>  <P>I did not want to repost such a large pic.  Anyway I could only find 2 Fiero's w/wing holes in the junkyard for reference and this was over 2 months and 4 different bone yards.  One was not factory, but in the same location as the other which I believe to be stock, due to the white marked trunk springs.  Sorry I forgot the years, but they were both notchbacks not GT.  Anyway I mounted mine where the red arrow points to the nub.  <P>Considering that you have an 88GT or any Fiero for that matter, I would wait and check out some other 88 GT’s at the show or hopefully someone here will know.<P>If indeed that is the correct location, this is how I did mine:<P>With the spoiler on the ground upside down measure the distance from where the rear screw ends protrude from the spoiler stands from left to right.  Then do the same from under the trunk from nub to nub; the measurements should be identical.  (Optional: Before drilling you can put some tape over your paint to keep it from chipping, but I found that it really did not make a difference.)  Next drill through the center of the outer rear nubs. To check for alignment place the spoiler onto the trunk.  The rear spoiler mounting screws should fit perfectly.  Now it's time for the front holes.  <P>To drill out the front holes, use some double sided tape, the white foamy type.  Cut the tape into some small pieces.  Place the pieces onto the spoiler’s gasket, the side that will face the trunk's surface.  Now, once again place the spoiler onto the trunk with the gaskets in place.  Since the rear mounting screws are just a bit longer than the front ones, place them into the previously drilled holes.  With the spoiler leveled, push the gasket down onto the trunk, the double-sided tape should hold it in place.  Remove the spoiler leaving the gasket stuck to the trunk as a template, drill out the front holes.  Now remove the gaskets and the double side tape.  Replace the gasket onto the spoiler and mount it.<P>I hope this helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> good luck!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Phantom Rage (edited 03-15-2001).]||||reg||7
